---
import SabGuide from '../../components/sab/SabGuide.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../lib/guides';
import { QUIZ_DURATION } from '../../lib/quiz';

const guide = getGuide('scholarships-for-grade-12-students-alberta');
---

<SabGuide guide={guide}>
  <p>
    Grade 12 scholarship season is not one deadline, it is a rolling series of them from September to June. Most students find out about the big fall awards in spring, after they have closed. This is the timeline that prevents that.
  </p>
  <p>
    The shape of the year is worth learning before the detail, because it is counterintuitive: <strong>the money gets smaller and the odds get better as the year goes on</strong>. The six-figure national awards are gone by Halloween. The $500 local award you can actually win is still open in May. A student who treats September like May loses the first group; a student who treats May like September burns their spring writing essays for competitions they were never going to win.
  </p>

  <h2>Before September: build the kit once</h2>
  <p>
    Almost every application in the year ahead asks for the same five things. Assembling them once, in August or the first week of September, is the single highest-leverage thing you can do, because it turns each later application from an evening's work into twenty minutes.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>An activity list.</strong> Every club, job, team, volunteer role and project, with dates, hours and what you actually did in it. Go back to Grade 10. You will not remember this in April.</li>
    <li><strong>Your transcript and current average.</strong> Know your number before an application asks for it.</li>
    <li><strong>Two referees who have said yes.</strong> Not "people who would probably say yes". The <a href="/templates/reference-letter/">reference letter template</a> is the exact message to send them.</li>
    <li><strong>One 500-word personal essay</strong> about something real that happened to you, written to be re-aimed rather than reused verbatim.</li>
    <li><strong>A list of the awards you are actually eligible for.</strong> <a href="/match/">The match quiz</a> takes {QUIZ_DURATION} and builds it for you; the <a href="/deadlines/">deadline calendar</a> shows you what is coming month by month.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>September and October: the big national awards open</h2>
  <p>
    The most valuable scholarships in Canada close earliest. The <a href="/guides/loran-award-guide/">Loran Award</a> (roughly $150,000 over four years) opens September 9 and closes October 15, and it needs a real application: essays, references, and activities going back years. If you are reading this in September of Grade 12, start Loran this week.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is not the only thing in the window. The <a href="/scholarships/advancing-futures-bursary/">Advancing Futures Bursary</a> for students who have been in government care closes at the start of October and is one of the largest single awards on this site. The <a href="/scholarships/rbc-ignite-scholarship/">RBC Ignite Scholarship</a> closes the same day, the <a href="/scholarships/atco-indigenous-education-high-school-merit-award/">ATCO Indigenous Education merit award</a> the day after, and three more (the <a href="/scholarships/keyera-energy-peter-j-renton-memorial-scholarship/">Keyera Peter J. Renton Memorial</a>, the <a href="/scholarships/anna-john-kolesar-memorial-scholarship/">Anna &amp; John Kolesar Memorial</a> and the <a href="/scholarships/tiessen-foundation-broadcast-scholarship/">Tiessen Foundation broadcast award</a>) all land on October 31. September itself closes on the <a href="/scholarships/charmaine-letourneau-scholarship/">Charmaine Letourneau Scholarship</a> and the two UFCW awards, which are open to members' families and are among the least-competed awards of the fall.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask two teachers for reference letters now, before the November crush. Our <a href="/guides/reference-letters-for-scholarships/">reference letter guide</a> covers exactly how.</li>
    <li>Write one strong personal essay you can adapt. See <a href="/guides/how-to-write-a-scholarship-essay/">how to write a scholarship essay</a>.</li>
    <li>Put every October date in your phone the day you find it, not the week it is due.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>November and December: community leadership and identity-based awards</h2>
  <p>
    Awards like <a href="/scholarships/td-scholarships-for-community-leadership/">TD Scholarships for Community Leadership</a> ($70,000, mid-November) and the <a href="/scholarships/wolf-scholars-program/">Wolf Scholars Program</a> ($80,000, early November) close in this window. These reward sustained volunteering over one-off achievements, which is exactly why they cannot be assembled in the last week.
  </p>
  <p>
    November 1 is also the densest single day of the fall for Alberta students: the <a href="/scholarships/first-nations-metis-and-inuit-bursary/">First Nations, Métis and Inuit Bursary</a>, the <a href="/scholarships/cenovus-energy-indigenous-scholarship/">Cenovus Energy Indigenous Scholarship</a>, <a href="/scholarships/indspire-building-brighter-futures-bursaries-and-scholarships/">Indspire's Building Brighter Futures</a> and the <a href="/scholarships/atb-financial-bursary/">ATB Financial Bursary</a> all close on it. If more than one of those applies to you, work backwards from November 1 in October, not in the last weekend.
  </p>
  <p>
    December is quieter and useful for it: the <a href="/scholarships/university-of-lethbridge-board-of-governors-award/">University of Lethbridge Board of Governors Award</a> and the <a href="/scholarships/canadian-natural-resources-scholarship/">Canadian Natural Resources Scholarship</a> both sit mid-month, and the holidays are the last long block of time you get before the spring. If your application is thin on sustained involvement, December is also the last month where starting something can still honestly be called sustained by the spring local awards.
  </p>

  <h2>January and February: institutional and nominated awards</h2>
  <p>
    University entrance scholarships mostly assess you automatically when you apply for admission, but the big named ones have winter internal deadlines: <a href="/scholarships/schulich-leader-scholarships/">Schulich Leader Scholarships</a> ($100,000, school nomination required) closes in late January, and the <a href="/scholarships/terry-fox-humanitarian-award/">Terry Fox Humanitarian Award</a> ($28,000) closes at the start of February. Two things to do:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask your guidance counsellor which awards your school can nominate students for, and when the internal deadline is. Internal deadlines are always earlier than published ones; for a nominated award like Schulich, the date that matters to you may be six weeks before the one published on the award's own site.</li>
    <li>Apply for admission early. Some entrance awards only consider students admitted by a cutoff date.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    February 1 is the winter's version of November 1: the <a href="/scholarships/alberta-masonic-higher-education-bursary/">Alberta Masonic Higher Education Bursary</a> and the <a href="/scholarships/kin-canada-bursary/">Kin Canada Bursary</a> both close on it. This is also where the first genuinely local deadlines appear, which is the signal that the season has turned.
  </p>

  <h2>March to May: local awards, where the odds are best</h2>
  <p>
    Spring is when city bursaries, service clubs, community foundations, and employer awards all close. Individually they are smaller, often $500 to $1,000, but the applicant pools are tiny. A Medicine Hat award competing against a few dozen local students is a far better bet than a national award competing against thousands. The <a href="/scholarships/lethbridge-county-community-benefit-scholarship/">Lethbridge County Community Benefit Scholarship</a> and the <a href="/scholarships/south-country-co-op-scholarship/">South Country Co-op scholarship</a> are both in this window.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is also the densest part of the year by volume (more awards close in May than in any other month) and the part most students sleepwalk through, because by May they have an acceptance letter and the urgency has drained out. It is the exact wrong moment to stop. Browse <a href="/scholarships/">the directory</a> filtered to your region, or go straight to your own city's page from the <a href="/scholarships/medicine-hat/">Medicine Hat</a>, <a href="/scholarships/calgary/">Calgary</a>, <a href="/scholarships/edmonton/">Edmonton</a>, <a href="/scholarships/lethbridge/">Lethbridge</a> and <a href="/scholarships/red-deer/">Red Deer</a> hubs, and read <a href="/guides/local-scholarships-better-odds/">why local awards are the best value</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>June and beyond: the Rutherford</h2>
  <p>
    After graduation, one more application is waiting: the <a href="/guides/alexander-rutherford-scholarship-guide/">Alexander Rutherford Scholarship</a>, worth up to $2,500 for your high school marks alone. You apply once you are enrolled in post-secondary. No essay, no competition. Do not forget it exists.
  </p>

  <h2>How many should you actually apply to?</h2>
  <p>
    More than you think, and fewer than a list of two hundred. The useful number is somewhere between eight and fifteen real applications across the year, weighted toward the ones where the applicant pool is small enough that a good application can win.
  </p>
  <p>
    The arithmetic is worth doing once. A $150,000 award drawing 5,000 applicants pays about $30 per application in expected value. A $1,000 local award drawing 30 applicants pays about $33, for a fraction of the work, and with no essay to write from scratch if you built the kit in September. That is not an argument against applying for Loran. It is an argument against spending your whole year on four awards you are competing for against the country while ignoring the ones in your own city that a few dozen people will apply for.
  </p>

  <h2>If you are reading this in March</h2>
  <p>
    Then the fall is gone, and that is genuinely fine. What is left is the better half for most students.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to the <a href="/deadlines/">deadline calendar</a> and read only the next two months. Everything else is noise right now.</li>
    <li>Pick every local and regional award you are eligible for. Do not filter by size yet.</li>
    <li>Write the one essay. Re-aim it for each award rather than rewriting it.</li>
    <li>Ask for references this week; three weeks' notice is the minimum, and in spring your teachers are being asked by everyone.</li>
    <li>Book the Rutherford reminder for your first month of post-secondary, and stop worrying about the awards that closed in October.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The one-line version</h2>
  <p>
    Big national awards in fall, nominations in winter, local awards in spring, Rutherford after graduation. Save the scholarships you plan to apply for and <a href="/saved/">turn on deadline reminders</a> so none of them pass silently.
  </p>
</SabGuide>
